e acceptance criteria and design decisions rather than its own requirement.\n\nMy final structure will have requirements that map directly to the SRD items, design decisions that cover the broader patterns like SIGPIPE handling and differential testing, and acceptance criteria that ensure compilability and test coverage.\n\nFor the task grouping, I'm organizing the SRD requirements across four tasks, with each task modifying the necessary files and building toward the complete implementation. Now I'll write out the final output with the project state summary included.\n\nActually, let me verify the constraint about task boundaries—it says not to split a single requirement across tasks, and since each requirement maps to exactly one task, that's satisfied. And regarding whether cmd/dirname counts as a \"new package\" that needs a contract task first, it doesn't exist in the codebase yet, so the constraint likely applies more to pkg/ packages rather than cmd/ executables. I'm realizing a contract task for a main package would just be an empty stub since there are no exported types or interfaces to define—it's pointless. So I'll skip it and move forward with the implementation tasks.\n\nNow I need to decide if I should tackle multiple utilities across different releases or focus solely on dirname. The constraint says to focus on the earliest incomplete release, which is rel02.0 with dirname. Since I need all four task slots for dirname's implementation, I won't have room to start on tee from rel02.1. So my final answer is four tasks for srd016-dirname.\n\nFor the required reading, I should point to the SRD itself, look at how basename is structured as a reference pattern, check the test utilities for RunDiffTests, and review the SIGPIPE handler. Since I can't verify if basename actually exists in the codebase, I'll be cautious about including it as required reading to avoid breaking anything.\n\nSo the core required reading is the SRD, the sys package for signal handli